  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Toronto buried them in the first inning and the Giants never recovered.",
    "what_this_means": "A 10-0 shutout is not a loss you spin into something larger. Webb threw seven strong innings. Five hits, two strikeouts. But the offense simply did not exist. Eleven strikeouts and a single hit. That is not a competitive effort. They trailed from the moment the game began and stayed there every inning after. This is what happens when the pitching shows up alone. The bullpen held clean through the final two innings, but there was nothing to hold onto by then. The game was decided before most lineups wake up.",
    "what_to_watch": "The consistency problem is not showing up on certain nights. It is showing up as a team that cannot score in any shape. Ramos reached once in four at-bats and that accounted for half the Giants' hits. The rest of the order struck out or disappeared. Down 5-0 before the second inning ended, they chased a game that was already over. This is the gap between a team that competes and a team that does not. When pitching is sound and the bat stays silent, there is no margin for error in a division race. Ten games back became eleven.",
